WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.820
Here is a puzzle. In the textbook model of an adult man, retain one litre of pure

2
00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:10.900
water, with no solute in it.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.820
About two thirds of that litre ends up inside cells.

4
00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:17.440
Now retain one litre of isotonic saline instead.

5
00:00:18.160 --> 00:00:21.400
Once it has spread, none of it ends up inside cells.

6
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.580
Same body, same volume.

7
00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:26.140
Why does the water go to different places?

8
00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:33.273
First, the tanks. Physiology texts use a 70 kilogram reference man, whose body is 60

9
00:00:33.273 --> 00:00:37.593
% water. That is a convention for arithmetic, not a measurement of anyone.

10
00:00:38.293 --> 00:00:40.633
Total body water is about 42 litres.

11
00:00:41.113 --> 00:00:46.853
About two thirds of it, 28 litres, is intracellular fluid, the water inside cells.

12
00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:52.613
About one third, 14 litres, is extracellular fluid, the water outside them.

13
00:00:53.317 --> 00:00:55.897
Water crosses most cell membranes quickly.

14
00:00:56.317 --> 00:01:00.137
So picture two rooms joined by a door that only water can pass.

15
00:01:00.657 --> 00:01:04.157
The guests stuck in each room decide how much water it holds.

16
00:01:04.837 --> 00:01:09.957
Inside cells, the stuck guests are anions that cannot leave, and the potassium that

17
00:01:09.957 --> 00:01:11.077
balances their charge.

18
00:01:11.737 --> 00:01:15.477
Outside, they are sodium, with chloride and bicarbonate.

19
00:01:16.077 --> 00:01:20.477
Sodium leaks into cells, but the sodium-potassium pump moves it back out.

20
00:01:20.477 --> 00:01:24.837
So sodium salts are the effective solute of the extracellular fluid.

21
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:27.040
Now count the guests.

22
00:01:27.500 --> 00:01:31.880
In the model, both rooms sit at 300 milliosmoles per kilogram.

23
00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:34.200
These numbers are made up, and rounded.

24
00:01:34.820 --> 00:01:36.840
Measured adult values run a little lower.

25
00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:41.220
28 litres of cells hold 8,400 milliosmoles.

26
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.800
14 litres of extracellular fluid hold 4,200 mOsm.

27
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.740
The model is a closed body, with no urine, sweat or breath losses, and added sodium

28
00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.600
chloride stays outside cells.

29
00:01:54.433 --> 00:01:56.873
Retain 1 litre of isotonic saline.

30
00:01:57.553 --> 00:02:01.313
Its particles stay outside, so the concentration does not change.

31
00:02:02.193 --> 00:02:03.973
Cells stay at 28 litres.

32
00:02:04.513 --> 00:02:06.613
Extracellular fluid grows to 15.

33
00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:09.953
Now retain 1 litre of solute-free water.

34
00:02:10.433 --> 00:02:17.013
In the model, it first sits outside cells, so extracellular fluid also reads 15.

35
00:02:17.653 --> 00:02:20.173
Then water crosses until both rooms match.

36
00:02:20.753 --> 00:02:24.253
They end at about 293 milliosmoles per kilogram.

37
00:02:25.193 --> 00:02:26.533
Cells gain two thirds of a litre.

38
00:02:27.233 --> 00:02:29.473
Extracellular fluid gains one third.

39
00:02:30.367 --> 00:02:33.527
The rule to keep: water follows effective solute.

40
00:02:34.087 --> 00:02:37.427
Each compartment holds water in proportion to its effective osmoles.

41
00:02:37.947 --> 00:02:43.367
In the model, retained saline stays in the extracellular fluid, because its sodium

42
00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:48.427
stays outside. Retained pure water is shared, two thirds to cells.

43
00:02:48.947 --> 00:02:50.667
This is equilibrium arithmetic.

44
00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:54.667
It predicts where a retained load goes, not how long it stays.
